isis will not be  stabilized till the time the country  finds some stability in the politics in  the government so that is what is  remaining right now  joining us is amber kasakunanathan  former human rights commissioner of sri  lanka thanks for joining the program  thank you for having me  i'm curious what happens next is anyone  really in charge  yes technically the president has still  not resigned  and uh while he is out of the country he  has  appointed the prime minister ranil  vikrama singha as acting president and  the speaker has also announced that the  president has informed him that during  the course of today he will submit his  resignation so if and when the president  submits his resignation  then automatically the prime minister  becomes the interim president and  they've already announced the date for  the reconvening of parliament which is  the 15th and the nominations to be filed  for parliament in parliament for the  president to be chosen by parliament  that's on the 19th and on the 20th they  will vote to elect the president who  will then serve out the rest of the term  of uh  you know the current of the current  president  now among the protesters in terms of  what they  want is their consensus uh moving  forward or there or is there a diversity  of opinions from people who've taken to  the streets for for months  well the protesters there are so many  different groups and there is a group  that has generally been you know for  over 90 days at uh gota go gamma in  colombo then there are various other  protests taking part in different parts  of the taking place in different parts  of the country  and the only consensus that we can see  until now is that they want the  president to resign and they want the  prime minister to resign at the moment  that appears to be the only consensus  and the main demand so as we speak there  are thousands of protesters outside uh  prime minister and well now acting  president rani will be promising his  office demanding that he stepped down  the the police have  used and the military have used water  cannons they have used tear gas against  them and the situation there right now  is quite tense  quite a fast developing situation i want  to take a step back i mean gotabaya  rajapaksa was part of a political  dynasty we've talked about this before  his brother was the former finance  minister who's also  fleeing other than their exile  will there be any accountability  from this family or do you think they  have escaped the law  well sri lanka doesn't have a good track  record with accountability  whether it is in relation to corruption  or in relation to wartime violations and  therefore whether in country any members  of this family will be held accountable  is doubtful  i feel that particularly given the  president uh involvement in wartime  violations the allegations against him  that there is probably a  higher likelihood of the president at  least being held accountable outside the  country than inside the country that's  very interesting something to watch uh  in the coming months  ambika sakunanathan thank you so much  for joining us as always  thank you very much  the political crisis is hitting citizens  at the pump with record high prices  in an effort to conserve fuel the  government is asking people to work from  home and has closed schools  restaurants have even switched to  cooking meals with wood  here's a closer look at how people are  enduring life in sri lanka  for seven days azam mohadin has been  waiting here in a kilometer long line  for gas in sri lanka's capital colombo  he'd heard a new fuel delivery was  coming he spent day and night here since  there is simply no more fuel  we have no more gas at home to cook  we used to have an income but for weeks  now the money has stopped coming  we can't go on like this  life is hard  to make matters worse his wife is ill  he tries to spend a little time with her  every day at lunchtime  risking losing his place in line  i keep calling him when he is there  waiting it's the tormenting thoughts  that have made me sick i was even  admitted to hospital  we didn't even have fuel for him to  drive me there  many people in sri lanka face similar  problems inflation is soaring and prices  are skyrocketing even for essentials  like food  hardly anyone can afford to buy what  they need  people used to have three meals a day  most of the time  now it's either two or one  sometimes people are drinking water  instead of eating  we don't know what to do what to give  our children they say they're hungry but  we don't have anything for them  we can't drink tea with milk anymore we  have no milk powder  we just about managed to buy rice but  even that is very expensive  another day has gone by in the line for  gas  and for azam  it's again been fruitless  his one hope that after the resignation  of president gotabaya rajapaksa better  times will finally come  only when we get problems like the fuel  crisis solved can we live a life worth  living  i haven't lost faith yet that a new  government will give us fresh hope and  change will finally happen  until then azam mohadin will spend  another night in line hoping that he  will finally get a few litres of fuel  you
 

